Vulnerability Title

Lack of Rate Limiting in Business Report Email Functionality Leading to Email Flooding

Risk Level

Moderate

CVSS v3.1 Score (Intranet Application)

5.3 – Medium

Vector String:
CVSS:3.1/AV:N/AC:L/PR:L/UI:N/S:U/C:N/I:L/A:L

Vulnerability Category

Improper Rate Limiting / Resource Abuse

OWASP Category

OWASP API Security – API4:2019 Lack of Resources & Rate Limiting
https://owasp.org/API-Security/editions/2019/en/0xa4-lack-of-resources-and-rate-limiting/

Vulnerability Description

The application contains a Business Report module which allows users to send selected reports to specified email addresses. During testing, it was observed that the email sending request can be captured and replayed multiple times without any restriction.

By intercepting the email request and using Burp Suite Intruder, the same request was repeatedly sent to the server more than 100 times. As a result, the application sent more than 100 emails to the specified email address without any validation, throttling, or rate limiting.

This indicates that the application lacks controls to prevent excessive email requests, allowing authenticated users to abuse the email functionality.

Impact

Attackers can flood user mailboxes with a large number of emails.

Can cause email spam and denial of mailbox service for recipients.

May lead to mail server resource exhaustion.

Could result in email domain reputation damage or blacklisting if abused at scale.

Potential misuse of internal infrastructure for spamming activities.

Proof of Concept (PoC) Steps

Step 1: Login to the application with valid credentials.

Step 2: Navigate to the Business Report module.

Step 3: Select a report and use the Send to Email functionality.

Step 4: Capture the request using Burp Suite Proxy.

Step 5: Send the captured request to Burp Intruder.

Step 6: Configure Intruder to repeat the request multiple times (e.g., 100+ requests).

Step 7: Start the attack.

Step 8: Observe that the recipient email address receives 100+ report emails generated by the application.

Recommendations

Implement rate limiting for email sending functionality.

Restrict the number of emails that can be sent per user within a defined time period.

Introduce CAPTCHA or additional validation for repeated email requests.

Implement server-side throttling and abuse detection mechanisms.

Log and monitor excessive email sending patterns to detect abuse attempts.

